 \"Reframe as large-scale field-level viability test with strong negative conclusions. Drop Claim 2 (decomposition) and Claim 3 (CaCLR method) as standalone claims. New angle: 'We conducted the first large-scale study testing whether English-trained correctness probes transfer cross-lingually across 3 architectures × 20 languages. Core finding: probe transfer is fundamentally limited by architecture-specific representation geometry that existing alignment methods (mean-shift, MMD, CORAL, linear projection) cannot reliably overcome. Control task analysis reveals that a substantial fraction of apparent shallow-layer transfer is capacity artifact, not genuine correctness signal.' Elevate the control task warning to a primary contribution (field-level methodological finding: all cross-lingual probing studies must include selectivity checks). Elevate the mean-shift impossibility proof (rank-invariance under StandardScaler + LR) as a clean theoretical contribution that clarifies confusion in the literature (Xiao et al.). Resource-tier analysis survives as descriptive empirical contribution. Linear projection's inconsistency itself is a finding — 'geometric alignment helps iff baseline representations are moderately aligned (0.50-0.60 AUROC), but the prediction of when this holds requires language-specific validation, undermining the zero-label promise.' Still targets EACL: negative results with scale (3 architectures × 20 langs), methodological contributions (control tasks, mean-shift proof), and constructive path-forward (few-shot 25-100 labels is the minimum viable approach).\"\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"c4a02af3-92f6-4f6c-a571-fdc2790e76b2","total_cost_usd":0.27550825,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18187,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":5706,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18187,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":